VulkanTransport

Independent static library built by transport/build.c with VulkanEngine.
Two separate transport backends are provided:

transport:: (legacy)
  Dense GPU patch-exchange prototype in transport.cpp and transport.comp.
  Quadratic storage and work. Retained for baseline comparisons.

transport::hierarchy::solver (new)
  hierarchy.hpp, hierarchy.cpp, shaders/hierarchy.comp
  CPU spatial hierarchy with adaptive cluster/patch interaction selection.
  GPU compressed row (CSR) exchange links, area-weighted cluster reduction.
  Physically reciprocal symmetric 2x2x2x2 quadrature for individual near
  patch pairs; approximate center exchange for distant source clusters.
  Adaptive visibility queries against oriented boxes at graph build time.
  Static multibounce diffuse transport using ping-pong GPU Jacobi passes.
  Direct bounce separated for high-resolution primary-light reconstruction.
  Changed-emission Neumann delta propagation without a dense rebuild.
  Residual checks with GPU atomic maximum and host-visible readback.
  A conditional upper bound on the discrete radiosity-solution error is
  calculated from the maximum transport row sum and surface reflectance.
  Discrete solve can continue with record_refine() to a chosen tolerance.
  Graph stats: surface/node/link counts, build ms, row sums, global scale,
  sampled reciprocity error, and actual discrete equation residual.

GPU compiler variants:
  glslangValidator -V -DHIER_SEED hierarchy.comp
  glslangValidator -V -DHIER_AGGREGATE hierarchy.comp
  glslangValidator -V -DHIER_GATHER hierarchy.comp
  glslangValidator -V -DHIER_EMISSION hierarchy.comp
  glslangValidator -V -DHIER_DIRECT hierarchy.comp
  glslangValidator -V -DHIER_INJECT hierarchy.comp
  glslangValidator -V -DHIER_PROPAGATE hierarchy.comp
  glslangValidator -V -DHIER_RESIDUAL hierarchy.comp

Important limits:
  This is an experimental adaptive hierarchical diffuse approximation,
  not exact GI or a new algorithm without prior art.
  Dense all-pair storage is avoided; finite-memory links are not proof
  of O(N) cost for every geometry and visibility arrangement.
  GPU node aggregation currently loops over patch ranges (O(N log N)
  work for balanced trees), not an optimized multilevel reduction.
  Geometry-change invalidation/rebuilds are not yet incremental.
  Visibility uses oriented boxes rather than full triangle BVH.
  Far-cluster visibility checks are sampled and not conservative.
  Global row scaling prevents amplification but is a biased safeguard.
  Surface reflectance is RGB Lambertian; specular, glass, volumes,
  spectral rendering, caustics and unbiased correction are absent.
  A low equation residual means convergence of the approximate
  operator, not physical correctness of every light transport path.
  Actual GPU timings and image error versus path tracing need measurements.
